    .{ .module = "erlang", .name = "processes", .arity = 0, .kind = .bif, .class = .justified, .reason = "deferred-bootset-pidvalues: E5.1 (Task 1) DISCHARGED the pid-representation-PRINT half — live pids are `.pid` TERMs printing the oracle's exact `<0.N.0>` shape (the E3.5 writer, pinned by the E5.1 pid-print homomorphism law), so the PRINT FORMAT is no longer the blocker. The RESIDUAL for processes/0 is the BOOT-SET: its pid NUMBER VALUES are the scheduler's 0,1,2… whereas the oracle's user pids carry the numbers the full preloaded SYSTEM-PROCESS set consumed first, so the enumerated list can only byte-match once that set boots. E5.2 made ALL 22 preloaded beams LOAD (DIVERGENCE 46/47 discharged) and E5.2b landed the code server, but boot-in-DEPENDENCY-ORDER (a running init + system-process set) stayed honestly infeasible in E5 (entry 69). E6.3 booted the purger/literal-collector HEAD of that set (2 system processes, boot.zig system_set_up), which is exactly why purge/litarea flipped. E7.4 EXTENDED that head to the kernel boot tree: 19 registered system processes (init, erl_prim_loader, the kernel/logger supervision trees, code_server, application_controller, ...) booted in dependency order (boot.zig boot_registered), and the registered-NAME SET is now byte-comparable and PROVEN a subset of the oracle's registered() (the boot-set name-differential law, exhaustively accounted against oracle_registered/boot_excluded). But processes/0 returns a pid-VALUE LIST, and THAT still cannot byte-match: the oracle boots 43 processes whose pid NUMBERS are per-node nondeterministic, and several (user_drv, standard_error, ... the boot_excluded port/driver set) are NOT processes this single-node interpreter can model — so the enumerated pid list is a genuine, honestly-named non-byte-EQ residual (the NAME set flipped comparable; the pid-VALUE list did not). True owner: boot-set pid-value parity / oracle process-topology parity, not an epoch bucket. (erts_internal:process_flag/3 was